  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on mean differences from a reference point (here, zero), which corresponds closely to expected value since the reference is zero. The individual also computed the expected gain and found it equal for both lotteries, aligning with the information used in the decision reason. However, the individual does not use the mean difference to make a choice (since they are indifferent on that basis) but instead invokes risk minimization as the decisive criterion. The decision reason, by contrast, would also be indifferent between the lotteries due to equal mean differences. While the individual considers the same initial information (expected value/mean difference), they go beyond the decision reason by applying a separate risk-minimization heuristic. The reason itself does not incorporate risk aversion or probability weighting—it only considers mean differences. Therefore, although the individual evaluates the relevant information, they do not apply the rule of the decision reason to make the choice; instead, they use a different rule. This suggests the decision reason was considered but not used to make the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
}

